# The Characteristics of Life

> What separates a living thing from a rock, a flame, or a crystal?
> Biologists answer with a checklist of processes — not a single magic
> property.

## 1. The Seven Characteristics

Something is generally considered alive when it shows all of the following:

| Characteristic | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Movement | Change of position or internal motion | Plants grow toward light |
| Respiration | Releasing energy from food | Mitochondria burning glucose |
| Sensitivity | Responding to the environment | Mimosa leaves folding when touched |
| Growth | Permanent increase in size/dry mass | A seedling becoming a tree |
| Reproduction | Producing new individuals | Bacteria dividing every 20 minutes |
| Excretion | Removing metabolic waste | Kidneys filtering urea |
| Nutrition | Obtaining organic matter for energy/materials | Photosynthesis; eating |

[definition]
**Metabolism** is the sum of all chemical reactions inside an organism —
the building-up (**anabolic**) and breaking-down (**catabolic**) processes
that make every characteristic above possible.
[/definition]

## 2. Tricky Cases

- **A virus** reproduces and evolves, but has no metabolism of its own
  and can't move or respond — most biologists class it as non-living.
- **A candle flame** moves, grows, consumes fuel and excretes smoke, but
  has no organisation, reproduction, or response from *within* — not alive.
- **A mule** cannot produce fertile offspring yet is unmistakably alive:
  reproduction counts at the level of the species, not the individual.

[important]
Life is defined by *processes*, organised in a bounded, self-maintaining
system. No single trait — movement, growth, even reproduction — is
sufficient on its own.
[/important]

## 3. Levels of Biological Organisation

Characteristics of life emerge as levels stack up:

@diagram tree id="levels-of-life" layout="tree" aria="Levels of biological organisation"
  Cell -> Tissue
  Tissue -> Organ
  Organ -> "Organ system"
  "Organ system" -> Organism
  Organism -> Population
  Population -> Community
  Community -> Ecosystem
@/diagram

## Example

A heartbeat is a property of no single muscle cell. Cells contract;
*only the organ* beats. This emergence of new properties at each level
is why biology studies everything from molecules to ecosystems.

## Key Takeaways

- The seven characteristics describe life by what living things *do*.
- Viruses fail the metabolism test; flames lack internal organisation.
- New properties appear at every level from cell to ecosystem.
